their unique needs and create long -lasting partnerships. The Group operates on a decentralised basis, enabling an entrepreneurial approach by local country management teams. Management believes that the Group has a strong financial foundation with a low level of leverage which means the Group can invest in growth, enabling its businesses to innovate their offer, and evolve their operating model. Management consider that the Group’s financial strength also attracts new clients seeking stability and long -term outsourcing solutions and that the Group’s scale enables its businesses to pass on purchasing benefits to clients and consumers by offering quality products at more attractive prices. Management also consider that purchasing from local and diverse suppliers and social enterprises enables greater reinvestment into local communities. The Group has targeted climate net zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050 alongside validated science-based targets to reduce emissions by 2030 (from a 2019 base year) in line with the Paris Agreement and has made reducing food waste a Group priority. The Group prioritises investment in capital expenditure to support organic growth in both new business wins and retention of existing contracts, and bolt-on M&A opportunities that strengthen its capabilities and broaden its exposure. People, Performance and Purpose The Group endeavours to manage its business with a disciplined focus on People, Performance and Purpose as its main strategic priorities. The People pillar is focused on ensuring the Group creates opportunities for its employees and positively impacts and represents the communities in which it operates. The Performance pillar is focused on delivering long-term valued relationships. The Group drives operational efficiency via the Management and Performance ("MAP") framework (see further below), while continuing to focus on delivering innovative, healthy and exciting food service solutions for clients and consumers. The Purpose pillar addresses the Group's social and environmental responsibilities. The Group continually aims to seek ways to be more socially and environmentally responsible. Market sectors The Group segments its markets into the following five sectors: Business & Industry ; Education; Healthcare & Senior Living; Sports & Leisure; and Defence, Offshore & Remote. The Group considers that its sector and sub -sector portfolio enables the operating businesses to focus on the different requirements of and to create bespoke solutions for its clients: 89 Business & Industry In this sector the Group works with a diverse range of clients including in the financial, legal, technology and manufacturing sectors. The Group’s scale, flexible operating models and digital capabilities help it to tailor its dining solutions to each client. In addition, the Group can deliver a range of hospitality-led services. Sector brands include: Eurest, Restaurant Associates, Bon Appétit, Flik, Canteen and ESFM. Healthcare & Senior Living In this sector the Group works directly with healthcare providers and aims to prepare food that improves patient and senior living experiences – from restaurant -style cafés to in -room patient dining and specialist feeding. The Group also provides a range of bespoke support services such as patient transportation and laundry and linen services. Sector brands include: Medirest, Crothall, Morrison and Unidine. Education Within the Education sector the Group provides healthy, balanced meals right through the learning journey, from nurseries to universities. The Group’s catering solutions come in multiple formats, from traditional onsite dining to vending and delivery or takeaway options. The Group can also provide tailored support service packages along with the dining solutions such as cleaning and waste management. Sector brands include: Chartwells, Bon Appétit, SSC and Flik. Sports & Leisure The Group aims to enhance customer experiences, providing food, beverages and hospitality across large stadiums, conference venues, museums and galleries. Sector brands include: Levy and Restaurant Associates. Defence, Offshore & Remote In the remote and offshore sector, the Group provides food and support services to many major oil, gas, mining and construction companies. The Group aims to provide uninterrupted support, however challenging the operating conditions. In the defence sector, the Group targets operations outside of areas of conflict. Sector brand: ESS Support Services Worldwide. Consumer brands The Group maintains and develops its portfolio of consumer -facing brands which have been acquired and scaled over time. To complement the Group's in-house brands in certain markets and countries, the Group leverages its scale to operate major "high-street" consumer brands from its clients' premises. These are normally operated on a franchise basis, which enables the Group to combine its market expertise and service skills with the consumer awareness of such brands. Clients As noted above, the Group has a diverse sector portfolio, and a wide ranging client base. Contracts with clients are principally based on a cost -plus, profit and loss or fixed price model. Input cost increases are mitigated wherever possible with some pricing passed on to clients under the various contract structures. The cost -plus model is the easiest to pass through price followed by the profit and loss model which enables the Group to change consumer prices subject to competitive pressure. Cost 90 increases under the fixed price contracts are typically linked to contractual indexation clauses which are reviewed at various intervals as specified in the client terms. Geographical Diversification The Group has a geographically and culturally diverse business with operations in around 30 countries in North America, Europe and Asia Pacific. The Group's operations are managed on a country-by-country basis . Until 30 September 2024, the Group reported performance through three regions: North America; Europe and Rest of World. Based on the twelve months ended 30 September 2024, North America accounted for 68 per cent. of underlying revenue, Europe accounted for 23 per cent. and Rest of World accounted for 9 per cent. With effect from 1 October 2024, the Group ’s internal management reporting structure has been changed to combine Rest of World with Europe to form a new International region. Management and Performance framework The Group uses its MAP framework to drive performance across the business. MAP is a simple framework which is embedded in the Group's culture and allows it to ensure all employees are focused on the same set of performance drivers, which are: Client Sales and Marketing (MAP1): This is about winning new business and retaining the Group's existing clients. The Group invests in sales and retention and is increasingly sectorising and sub - sectorising its businesses around the world to allow it to get closer to clients. Consumer Sales and Marketing (MAP 2) : Like-for-like revenue consists of both volume and price, the Group is focused on attracting and satisfying its client base with strong consumer propositions. Cost of Food (MAP 3): Food makes up around one-third of the Group's costs. In addition to the benefits of its scale in food procurement, the Group aims to manage food costs through careful menu planning and by rationalising the number of products it buys. In-unit Costs (MAP 4): In-unit costs are made up predominantly of labour. By using labour scheduling techniques and improving productivity, the Group aims to deliver the optimum level of service in the most efficient way. Above-unit Overheads (MAP 5): The Group has a simple organisational model with few layers of management and little bureaucracy, which enables it to keep overheads low whilst it continues to aim to grow revenue. The MAP framework has been highly successful in delivering and improving financial performance. Market Opportunity Based on Management estimates, the Group believes that the addressable global food services market is worth approximately U.S.$320 billion, of which the Group has less than a 15 per cent. market share. The Group further believes that this provides a significant runway for growth, with nearly three-quarters of the market still self-operated or in the hands of regional players. Administrative, Management and Supervisory Bodies Name and Functions The Board of Directors is made up of twelve members, comprising the Chair of the Board, three executive directors, a senior independent director and seven non-executive directors. The Board is responsible for the performance and long -term success of the Issuer, including health and safety, leadership, strategy, values and standards, controls and risk management and may, subject to the Articles of Association and applicable legislation, borrow money, guarantee, indemnify, mortgage or charge the business, property, assets (present and future) and issue debentures and other securities and give security, whether outright or as a collateral security, for any debt, liability or obligation of the Issuer or of any third-party. 91 As at 12 June 2025, the members of the Board of Directors of the Issuer are: Name Function Principal other activities outside the Issuer Ian Meakins Chair of the Board Non-executive director and Chair of Unilever PLC. Dominic Blakemore Group Chief Executive Officer Non-executive director of London Stock Exchange Group plc. Vice -chair of the Council of University College London and chair of the board of trustees of FareShare. Petros Parras Group Chief Financial Officer None. Palmer Brown Group Chief Operating Officer, North America None. Anne-Françoise Nesmes Senior Independent Director Non-executive director of Sanofi. Liat Ben-Zur Designated Non -Executive Director for Workforce Engagement Independent director of Talkspace, Inc. and Splashtop Inc. Also advises start -ups through her own consultancy firm, LBZ Advisory. St